parser_bison: close the IGMP scanner scope when igmp is used as a protocol value

The scanner unconditionally pushes the SCANSTATE_IGMP flex start condition
on the "igmp" keyword.  In the primary_rhs_expr protocol-value production
every protocol alternative pops its scanner scope again via close_scope_*
(TCP, UDP, UDPLITE, ESP, AH, ICMP, ICMP6, GRE, COMP, DCCP, SCTP) - except
IGMP, which is missing close_scope_igmp.

Consequence: every ruleset containing e.g.

    meta l4proto igmp counter accept

(the standard fw4 "Allow-IGMP" rule) permanently leaks one start-condition
stack entry, leaves the scanner's startcond_type stuck at SCANSTATE_IGMP,
and via the deferred-pop bookkeeping in scanner_pop_start_cond() also loses
the pop of the following scope (e.g. the counter scope).  The corrupted
bookkeeping accumulates across the ruleset and, depending on the following
content, ends in the fatal flex error

    start-condition stack underflow

which aborts the parse.  On our RTL9607F ONU image this made the whole
fw4-rendered ruleset unloadable (no firewall, no masquerade, LAN->WAN
dead).

Add the missing close_scope_igmp, matching all sibling alternatives.
Verified with an instrumented scanner: the fw4 ruleset now ends with a
balanced start-condition stack (depth 0, no deferred pops) instead of two
leaked entries and a stale IGMP state.

--- a/src/parser_bison.y
+++ b/src/parser_bison.y
@@ -5388,7 +5388,7 @@
 							 BYTEORDER_HOST_ENDIAN,
 							 sizeof(data) * BITS_PER_BYTE, &data);
 			}
-			|	IGMP
+			|	IGMP	close_scope_igmp
 			{
 				uint8_t data = IPPROTO_IGMP;
 				$$ = constant_expr_alloc(&@$, &inet_protocol_type,
